=== #1545 body:
Part of #655

`comp:api` · **S**

Found by the `/security-review` and `/crypto-privacy-review` gates on the PR that closes #1534. That PR split the challenge pools per operation. One pair stays fused.

**Problem.** `POST /auth/siwe/login` and `POST /auth/identity/wallet` both consume kind `siwe-login`, both demand `SIWE_LOGIN_STATEMENT`, and both take their nonce from the one unauthenticated mint at `POST /auth/siwe/challenge`. A holder of one signed sign-in message therefore picks the route.

The two routes do not pay the same. `/auth/siwe/login` answers with a session on an already-linked wallet. `/auth/identity/wallet` answers with a CipherBox identity token, which the Core Kit turns into the derived account key ([ADR 0008](https://github.com/FSM1/cipher-box-next/blob/main/decisions/0008-identity-tokens-are-minted-by-the-api.md) D1/D2). The second is strictly more than the first.

**Why it is not urgent.** `/auth/siwe/login` has no production caller: the live wallet sign-in is `/auth/identity/wallet` (`apps/web/src/components/auth/WalletLoginButton.tsx`), and the engine's `Command::SiweLogin` reaches no UI. No legitimate prompt produces a signature aimed at the weaker route, so the escalation has no live source today. It is filed because the invariant the split establishes — one pool per operation — must hold for every pair, and because a future caller of `/auth/siwe/login` would make the source live.

**Two candidate fixes; decide before implementing.**

1. **Split the pair.** Add kind `siwe-identity`, its own statement, and its own unauthenticated mint. Touches `apps/api`, `packages/login` (`walletNonce`), and the statement copy in `apps/web`.
2. **Delete the weaker route.** Remove `POST /auth/siwe/login`, `ApiClient::siwe_login` and `Command::SiweLogin` if nothing is to call them in v2.0. The pair then cannot be confused because only one route exists. Confirm the desktop shell has no plan for it first.

**AC sketch.**

- A wallet sign-in message and signature accepted at one of the two surfaces is refused at the other, proven by an API integration test in each direction — or the second surface no longer exists.
- No open mint feeds two operations whose outcomes differ in strength.

`blocked_by`: none. Coordinates with #1534, which established the per-operation pools this completes.
